  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/c8/Avengers_Vol_1_5.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The story picks up immediately after the conclusion of <i>Fantastic Four</i> #26, and the Avengers return home to inspect the damage caused to their meeting place by the Hulk.&nbsp; A construction crew is summoned while the various Avengers head back to their respective homes to take care of personal matters, Rick Jones heading off with Captain America, who promises to teach him some acrobatic moves.\n\nBut only \"a few days later,\" at Stark's Flushing, Long Island, plant, a mysterious sound is detected in the earth.&nbsp; It is also heard inside an anthill that Giant-Man and the Wasp are investigating.&nbsp; And Don Blake is at that very moment reading a newspaper article about strange sounds causing a train accident.&nbsp; It's happening everywhere, so Thor decides to summon the Avengers.&nbsp; He personally goes to collect Captain America, and soon, the Avengers learn that the strange sounds are originating out in the southwestern part of the country.\n\nSure enough, out near where the Hulk is often seen, a strange mound has General Ross mystified, even as Dr. Bruce Banner finally returns to the installation after months.&nbsp; Ross asks him to look into the mystery.\n\nThe reader is finally shown the cause.&nbsp; The Lava Men are pushing \"living rock\" up toward the surface world.&nbsp; Somehow this will menace the \"surface men\" and save the Lava Men.&nbsp; Only one of them objects, the \"Lava Man\" whom Thor faced in last August's <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> 97 ([LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16]), who is given the name \"Molto\" here.&nbsp; The Witch Doctor seems intent on \"war between us and the surface people!\"&nbsp; The leader of the Lava Men ignores Molto's advice to stop.\n\nOn the surface, just before General Ross can attack the mound (which is apparently \"living rock\" being forced to the surface so that it doesn't endanger the Lava Men), the Avengers arrive on the scene.\n\nIron Man begins by testing the rock with his \"repulsers\" [sic] (yes, spelled like this), which is felt below by the Lava Men, and they think they're being attacked!&nbsp; Iron Man is overwhelmed by the heat the Lava Men put out, and Thor arrives to help, apparently immune to the intense heat.&nbsp; He addresses the ruler.\n\nThor learns that the \"living rock\" is growing bigger each day and emits a noise that damages anything it strikes, so these Lava Men are attempting to push it up to the surface world!&nbsp; The ruler reveals that they found it a long time ago when it was small, and that any attempt to strike it caused catastrophic explosions.&nbsp; It has grown to a size now where any attack on it could destroy the entire Earth, so the Lava Men are pushing it up to the surface in the hope that, if it explodes, most of the destruction will blow out into space, leaving them safe below the surface!&nbsp; That will have the additional side effect of leaving the surface world defenseless, allowing them to emerge and take over the planet.\n\nThor knows he dare not strike the stone, and the Witch Doctor then orders the Lava Men to head to the surface and attack!&nbsp; Meanwhile, Giant-Man and the Wasp have been studying the stone and have identified one weak spot where it might crack without exploding.&nbsp; They direct Thor to strike that very spot with his hammer.\n\nBut at that moment, Bruce Banner looks on, then reverts to the Hulk.&nbsp; He sees his enemies, the Avengers, and attacks!&nbsp; At the same time, the Witch Doctor attacks Thor, preventing him from getting to the rock.&nbsp; Instead, the Avengers manipulate the Hulk into striking the one weak spot in the rock, which <i>implodes</i> as a result instead of <i>exploding</i> -- the Earth is saved!\n\nThor commands the Lava Men to return to their homes...<i>or else</i>!&nbsp; He seals up the entrance to their underground realm.&nbsp; The Avengers pick themselves up and find that the Hulk has vanished yet again, and Betty finds Banner lying nearby.&nbsp; And as the Avengers discuss their apparent victory, an urgent summons suddenly comes in from the Teen Brigade.&nbsp; It's a \"condition red\" emergency!&nbsp; The Avengers race off!\n\n(And once again, I guess the Hulk's whereabouts are just not important enough to give a second thought to.&nbsp; <i>Wow!</i>)",
  "grade_one_liner": "The issue keeps introducing ideas that sound more interesting than the story being told.&nbsp; One clever ending twist can't compensate for the slog required to reach it.",
  "subtitle": "The Slow Burn of Banner Fatigue",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | SlowStart | width=20%] As nice as it was to see the Avengers returning from their encounter with the Hulk and Fantastic Four (in last month's [LINK:1964:FF_26|FF #26|2027-06-05]), and as nice as it <i>also</i> was to get a glimpse of each member out doing their own thing, it served very little purpose other than to burn through six pages of story.\n[BR]\n[BR] The Avengers didn't get back together to investigate the \"strange sounds\" until the top of page 7.&nbsp; Not only is this a [SPAN: severity=error; text=<i>remarkably</i> relaxed pace,] but, <i>wouldn't you know it</i>?&nbsp; It also just <i>happens</i> to be out in the Southwest.\n\n[ASSET: right | BannerIsBack | width=15%] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=That puts it <i>squarely</i> in the Hulk's old stomping grounds!]\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=But wait!] Didn't we just see Banner floating in the harbor at the end of last month's FF issue?\n[BR] How did he suddenly get out West again?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I guess page 3 <i>does</i> say \"a few days later...\"&nbsp; Yeah, that's <i>gotta</i> be it!&nbsp; Betty Ross is even <i>thrilled</i> at his return!)]\n\nBetween General Ross's rant about what a \"lily-livered milksop\" he is, and Banner's own reflections about what it is secretly like being the Hulk, Jack manages to consume yet <i>another</i> two pages, meaning that the gamma-inflicted scientist doesn't get to the matter of the mysterious hill poking up out of the ground until the last panel of page 8.\n\nOnly then is the reader shown what is causing the surface disruption!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=It's the Lava Men!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Gasp!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | MoltoOverruled | width=15%] The one Lava Man that had actually <i>been </i>to the surface before (given the name \"Molto\" here), the one who battled Thor back in August 1963's [LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16] -- this <i>one guy</i> seems to offer the only word of caution regarding their course of action.&nbsp; This is quite honestly [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=surprising], <i>especially</i> when one glances back at the aforementioned issue and finds language such as \"I must lead my people to the surface, so that we may take possession of all the Earth!\" (see its page 10).\n\nBut <i>hey</i> -- Stan and Jack aren't likely to let a small matter such as <i>continuity</i> stop them from extracting yet another 12 cents from their readers!&nbsp; [SOUNDEFFECT: severity=success; text=KA-CHING!]\n\nThe <i>important</i> thing is getting all that \"living rock\" out from under the earth so that it menaces the <b>surface people</b> and not the Lava Men!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Also, are there any Lava <i>Women</i>?)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe Avengers finally arrive on the scene, and once there, Iron Man says he'll investigate first.\n[ASSET: center | AvengersHaveArrived | width=large]\nAnd even though Captain America has only been with the team for <b>five minutes</b> (<i>okay</i>, it <i>has</i> been at least \"a few days\"), he tells Rick, \"We don't question each other's commands!&nbsp; Iron Man must have his reasons!\"\n\nHaving Iron Man rush forward into solo action buys Jack <i>another</i> two pages, followed then by Thor, who is the only Avenger who can withstand the intense heat of the Lava Men's realm.&nbsp; It is <i>he</i> who learns the origin of the \"Living Stone\" that threatens them.\n\n[ASSET: left | ManyMoonsAgo | width=36%] The Lava Men's ruler begins his story by saying it happened \"many moons ago,\" a curious phrase for a race that hasn't seen the moon in centuries (as implied by the comment made by one of them on page 12).\n\nPlease understand that I'm not saying this is <i>poor</i>, but little things like this prove to be [SPAN: severity=error; text=incredibly distracting] and ultimately lessen the intended impact of the storytelling.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head...)]\n\nThe flashback sequence never adequately explains why moving the stone is somehow safer than leaving it where it was originally discovered, but a moment later, [SPAN: severity=error; text=none of that matters,] because even though the so-called \"living stone\" has been shoved upward by the Lava Men to endanger the surface world (rather than themselves), the Witch Doctor still orders their army to <i>attack</i>!\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=Given the stated plan of action they had <b><i>just</i></b> relayed to Thor, this <i>literally</i> makes no sense at all!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Why attack when you can just <i>wait for the explosion</i>?&nbsp; I don't get it...)]\n\nThe reader is left to conclude that this rash course of action came about for no other reason than the story had now advanced to page 15, and as yet, the Avengers hadn't actually <i>done anything</i>!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And we can't have <i>that</i> now, can we?)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; text=Commence multi-page battle!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=I suppose I don't need to remind you that the Hulk is still around!&nbsp; Bet it won't be long before <i>he</i> shows up!]\n\nSure enough, even though the Lava Men's Witch Doctor keeps Thor occupied so that he can't hit the living stone's one weak spot that Giant-Man manages to find, Bruce Banner <i>also</i> has solved the mystery, only to [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=randomly become the Hulk], ultimately allowing the other Avengers to trick him into hitting the rock <i>for </i>them:\n[ASSET: center | NoHammerNoProblem | width=large]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Thank heavens doing this saves the Earth, causing the rock to <b><i>im</i></b><i>plode</i> rather than <b><i>ex</i></b><i>plode</i>.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Also, I find it remarkable that the Lava Men spent <i>years</i> fearing the stone, yet Giant-Man identifies its one safe weak spot almost immediately!)]\n\n[ASSET: left | StandingAround | width=13%] [ASSET: right | BannerOnceMore | width=19%] That leaves time for Thor to threaten the Lava Men into retreating before he heads back and learns that his teammates used the Hulk to eliminate the threat in his absence.&nbsp; Thor may find it \"odd how fate arranged for the Hulk to unwittingly come to the aid of mankind,\" but I call it just another case of [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Comic Book Convenience 101].\n[BR]\n[BR] The [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved,] Betty Ross finds the disheveled Banner \"a short distance away,\" and the Avengers are summoned back to NYC by the Teen Brigade, all in time for their next adventure!\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt is simply astonishing how many ridiculous elements there were in this story.&nbsp; It wasn't so much the artwork, as [SPAN: severity=success; text=Jack Kirby's presentation was largely adequate] (if admittedly not <i>inspired</i>).&nbsp; It's just the [SPAN: severity=error; text=vast number of inconsistencies or conveniences,] not to <i>mention</i> the unimaginative opponents... these \"Lava Men.\"\n\nThe story was most definitely the problem, and there's no telling whether it was Stan Lee directing the action, or whether it was Jack Kirby's page count padding and reliance on Stan to have it all make sense.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=No matter who is to blame, the story suffered from a host of problems.]\n\nI didn't like the fact that an action caused by the Lava Men's Witch Doctor actually resulted in Thor changing back to Don Blake.&nbsp; That strikes me as impossible given the hammer's enchantment.&nbsp; But even that wasn't enough for Stan and Jack, because one look at the living rock by Bruce Banner caused him to become the Hulk!&nbsp; And then one <i>implosion</i> later... <b>PRESTO!</b>  He's back to Banner.\n\nEven though the use of the Hulk to strike the living stone's weak spot [SPAN: severity=success; text=was actually the one good moment in the story,] it was clumsily executed and surrounded by poor storytelling.&nbsp; Furthermore, since his book was canceled, Stan just can't seem to help himself -- [SPAN: severity=info; text=he keeps having the Hulk appear almost every month.]&nbsp; (He can't even seem to bring himself to remove the Hulk's head from the upper-left cover logo, even though he <i>quit the team</i> three issues ago!)\n\nI simply do not understand this.\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=To me, the Hulk is little more than a walking plot device.]\n[HANG] Need a scientist?&nbsp; [SPAN: fonttype=bold; text=Bring in Banner!]\n[HANG] Need a plot?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=Fight the Hulk!]\n[HANG] Need someone to punch?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=Hulk!]\n[HANG] Need a monster whenever the plot demands?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=(You get the idea...)]\n[HANG] Need him to be nearby?&nbsp; [SPAN: fonttype=bold-italic; text=He's everywhere!&nbsp; He's everywhere!] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(just like Chickenman from the old radio show)]\n\n[LINK:1964:FF_26|FF #26|2027-06-05] left him floating in the water near New York City.&nbsp; But <i>of course</i>, now he's out in the Southwest!\n\nHe's even on the cover of this magazine, looking as if he's standing alongside the Avengers as they prepare to do battle with the Lava Men!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=That never happened in this story!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's hard to pass this off as creative license!)]\n\nNot only does Banner <i>randomly </i>turn into the Hulk on cue as the battle rages, but one mighty blow -- followed by a sudden implosion -- seems to be all it takes to turn him back to Banner!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=That makes no sense either.]&nbsp; I would imagine it would only make him <i>angrier</i>!&nbsp; It just goes to show how this character is repeatedly pulled in only as demanded by the plot, which is, in the end, not a good enough reason to include him.\n\nBut enough ranting about the Hulk...\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This <i>is</i> supposed to be <b><i>affectionate</i> </b>mockery, and I've most assuredly crossed the line this time...)]\n\nThere's just no getting around it:&nbsp; this was a lackluster story from start to finish and involved a dull opponent with a very weak backstory and rationale for their attack on the surface world.\n\nAnd as for \"villains\" such as the Lava Men -- <b><i>ugh</i></b> -- these races of people that live below the surface [SPAN: severity=error; text=stretch believability,] particularly since they don't ever seem to run into each other (at least not yet).\n\nWe have:\n[HANG] * The Lava Men right <i>here</i> (the second appearance of their race, the first being [LINK:1963:JIM_97_thor0|JIM #97|2026-12-16])\n[HANG] * The Mole Man's kingdom of Subterranea (see [LINK:1963:FF_22|FF #22|2027-02-17])\n[HANG] * Tyrannus (from [LINK:1962:HULK_5_hulk1|HULK #5])\n[HANG] * The Sub-Mariner, who has never been shy about his grievances with the surface world\n[HANG] * The descendants of the people of Atlantis that Iron Man bumped into (in [LINK:1963:TOS_43_iron0|TOS #43]).\n\nThose are just the few that I can remember offhand.\n[BR] And naturally, <i>all</i> of them seem to want to invade the surface world!\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Don't we have <i>enough </i>problems with aliens from far-off galaxies?&nbsp; Why compound that with people from <i>under</i> the earth?)]\n\n[HANG0] There's just no escaping it:&nbsp; if this issue teaches us <i>anything</i>, it's that\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=The <i>Avengers</i> is still a work-in-progress!]",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Jack Kirby <i>continues</i> to draw Iron Man flying with his <i>knees bent</i>, both on the cover and on pages 10 and 11\n[BR] -- [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=it's maddening!]\n\n[HANG0] The Avengers arrive via helicopter <i>again</i> out in the Southwest.\n[BR] I commented on the impracticality of this during my review of [LINK:1964:FF_25|FF #25|2027-05-10]."
  ],
  "letters_page": "This issue introduces the letters page (called simply \"All About the Avengers\") and contains a letter from future comics creator Alan Weiss.",
  "highlight": "The Lava Men return with a world-ending threat, but the real surprise is how quickly the Hulk is becoming the Swiss Army Knife solution to the Avengers' every problem."
}